dash='## Quant & Market Dashboard\n\n### Liquidity and volatility\n\n- **Overnight:** Current ES/NQ and ETF spreads, depth, overnight volume, Treasury liquidity and options positioning are unverified. The futures rebound is a direction proxy, not evidence of easy execution.\n- **Prior-close activity, November 5:** completed daily rows show SPY 733.00, volume 125.89m, and QQQ 647.05, volume 130.68m. Quote banners differ from daily rows; use the latter consistently and reconcile against the execution feed before trading. Heavy volume is not narrow spreads or available depth. [SPY](https://chartexchange.com/symbol/nyse-spy/historical/) · [QQQ](https://chartexchange.com/symbol/nasdaq-qqq/historical/).\n- **Funding:** latest verified November 4 SOFR 3.94% versus EFFR 3.88%, a six-basis-point gap. FRED updated November 5 at 7:02 and 8:02 a.m. CST respectively. SOFR fell three basis points from November 3; these lagged fixings do not establish current Treasury or funding stress. [SOFR](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SOFR) · [EFFR](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/EFFR).\n- **Regime: High; medium confidence.** Delayed VIX 26.44 at November 6 5:36 a.m. CST, versus November 5 close 28.18 and November 4 close 16.61. House thresholds: below 15 low, 15–25 moderate, 25–35 high, above 35 stressed. This is an explicit operating convention, not a calibrated forecast. Skew, term structure and dealer gamma remain unknown. [VIX](https://chartexchange.com/symbol/index-vix/historical/).\n\n### Factor proxies\n\n| Factor | ETF | Nov 4→5 | Oct 8→Nov 5 (4 weeks) |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n'+rows+'\nSource-linked Close columns on a common provider scale; price returns exclude distributions and are not isolated factor premia. Corporate-action histories were not independently refreshed. Do not splice these levels into differently adjusted series. All six proxies fell on the day; low volatility lost less and remained positive over four weeks. Rounded-close calculations can differ from provider percentage columns.\n\n### Execution and microstructure\n\n- Jobs release **7:30 a.m. CST / 13:30 UTC**; cash opening auction **8:30 a.m. CST / 14:30 UTC**. Refresh state after each rather than carrying pre-release quotes into orders.\n- For SPY/QQQ, inspect quote age, NBBO spreads, displayed depth, auction imbalances and ES/NQ basis. Reduce participation when depth thins; use bounded limits, cancellation controls and realistic slippage budgets. A rising price on thin depth is a liquidity trap.\n- For options, verify expiry, deliverables and settlement; reject stale, crossed and one-sided quotes, and price packages coherently. Friday expiry and short-dated gamma can accelerate hedging flows, but dealer-gamma sign, pinning and flow magnitude are unverified.\n- Recalibrate impact estimates after the volatility jump. Treat missed fills, gaps, latency and spread expansion as separate stress dimensions.\n\n**Research watchlist:** SPY, QQQ, IWM, RSP, TLT; ES, NQ, ZN, VX; MTUM, VLUE, QUAL, IWF, SPLV; ABNB, SOXX.\n\n### DRL Improvements for the MATLAB System\n\n1. Enforce drawdown, exposure, participation and stale-data limits outside the policy, with a safe fallback.\n2. Penalize spread, impact, turnover, latency and missed fills; report tail losses and risk breaches separately from reward.\n3. Lock feature and reward choices before a purged walk-forward test; use a one-use final holdout and retain the full trial ledger.\n4. Stress unseen high-volatility regimes with widened spreads and delayed observations; require calibrated uncertainty or abstention before restoring normal risk.\n\n### Model hygiene\n\nAudit leakage, survivorship bias, look-ahead, corporate actions, stale options, multiple testing, calibration and drift. Preserve point-in-time universes, economic vintages and availability timestamps. Fit preprocessing only on training folds; never recycle an inspected final holdout.\n\n### MATLAB optimization patterns\n\n- Align named-zone timetables causally with synchronize/retime; do not backfill unreleased data.\n- Vectorize rolling features, preallocate logs and profile the actual bottleneck.\n- Parallelize independent folds or Bayesian trials with reproducible random streams and an explicit search budget.\n- Use GPUs only for suitable batches; keep hypothesis proposals separate from trusted risk and evaluation modules.\n\n### High-value quant research question\n\n**Does requiring a frozen trial ledger and one-use holdout reduce false strategy acceptance compared with repeatedly selecting the best validation score?**\n\nCompare acceptance and subsequent performance under matched data, search budgets, costs and seeds. It tests research-process reliability rather than another unconstrained alpha search.\n'
(P/'dashboard.md').write_text(dash)
